  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry through midday Thursday. Isolated-\nscattered showers/storms return later Thursday into Thursday night, \nwith the highest chances expected Friday afternoon-Friday night with \na cold front. \n\nCurrent composite radar looks very similar to the past few days, \nwith an MCS to our NW and mostly clear across our area. Satellite \nimagery is showing low-level cu developing across most of the area, \nbut only one shower has managed to develop in NE NC as the best \ninstability is found near the coast working in conjunction with the \ntypical summertime sea-breeze boundaries in that area. Temperatures \nare currently in the low-mid 80s and dew points are in the 60s to \nlower 70s, making for a more comfortable August afternoon. Another \nfast moving shortwave dives E-SE in the flow aloft tonight, and \ncould set off a few showers, mainly across the far north and eastern \nshore. Lows tonight mostly in the lower 70s with a few pockets of \nupper 60s possible in the interior rural areas. \n\nThe region will remain in a similar pattern through the end of the \nweek, with NW flow aloft continuing. Light winds will lift to the \nsouth tonight and more humid conditions will return, with dew points \nof the upper 60s to lower 70s bringing the mugginess back to the \nregion. Most areas will see the heat index climb into the upper 90s \nto lower 100s as temperatures reach the lower to mid 90s. The \nhighest heat indices will be in NE NC, but they will likely fall \njust short of Heat Advisory criteria, so have decided not to issue \nany heat products for that area at this time. Still not a lot of \nfocus for storms Thursday, so PoPs will remain capped at 20-40%. SVR \nWeather is not anticipated as shear remains rather weak so any \nstorms should be disorganized. On Friday, the that will have been \nlingering to our north for a majority of the week is forecast to \nfinally drop south into the area as the flow aloft further amplifies \nin response to a strong ridge aloft across the lower MS Valley and a \nsharper trough over New England and eastern Canada. This will likely \nbe our best chance of rain for the remainder of the week. \nInstability ahead of the front may be limited on Friday, especially \nfor NE portions of the FA, so even with decent shear, the SVR threat \nis uncertain/low and at this time, and SPC does not even have the \nregion in a Marginal SVR risk. Locally heavy rainfall will be \npossible given highs PWATs but storms should be moving so unless the \nfront stalls, a widespread heavy rain event is currently not \nexpected (but with localized flooding possible, mainly in urban or \nflood-prone areas).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and slightly less humid Saturday,\nseasonable Sunday with a few afternoon showers/storms.\n\nSaturday looks mostly dry and fairly pleasant with highs in the 80s \nin the wake of the cold front, though more clouds and an isolated \nshowers/storm will be possible in NE NC. The upper ridge starts to \nbuild back into the region Saturday night/Sunday, and some of the \nmodels even depict showers Saturday night. Scattered shower and \nisolated thunderstorms will return to the forecast on Sunday, along \nwith highs back around 90F for most of the area. The expansive upper \nridge across the southern U.S. will finally start to break down and \nflow aloft will become more zonal. The current forecast currently \nhas the normal diurnally driven afternoon/evening isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms.",
